---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';
import { siteFacts } from '../../content/site-facts';
const taguette = 'https://www.taguette.org/';
const qual = 'https://github.com/ccbogel/QualCoder/blob/3.8.2/README.md';
---
<GuideLayout heading="Compare free qualitative analysis software" lead="Fleuron, Taguette and QualCoder offer different ways to work without a software license fee. Start with the kind of material you need to code and how you need to share it." sources={[{ label: 'Taguette: features', url: `${taguette}about.html` }, { label: 'Taguette: local and hosted installation', url: `${taguette}install.html` }, { label: 'QualCoder 3.8.2 documentation', url: qual }, { label: 'QualCoder releases (3.8.2 latest stable, 4.0 beta)', url: 'https://github.com/ccbogel/QualCoder/releases' }, { label: 'QualCoder 3.8.2 release notes (Taguette project import)', url: 'https://github.com/ccbogel/QualCoder/releases/tag/3.8.2' }, { label: 'QualCoder: Working Together in a Team', url: 'https://qualcoder.org/doc/en/2.4.-Working-in-a-Team/' }, { label: 'NVivo: Create a new project (local .nvp storage)', url: 'https://help-nv.qsrinternational.com/20/win/Content/projects-teamwork/create-a-new-project.htm' }, { label: 'MAXQDA: Where should I save the project file?', url: 'https://help.maxqda.com/en/support/solutions/articles/80001146630-where-should-i-save-the-project-file-' }, { label: 'Fleuron source and documentation', url: siteFacts.sources.repository }]}>
  <p class="note"><strong>Written by Fleuron’s developer.</strong> This is a maintainer comparison, not an independent review. Competitor details below come from their linked documentation; I have not performed a feature-by-feature test of those tools. The QualCoder column uses the 3.8.2 documentation, still the latest stable release as of this review (a <a href="https://github.com/ccbogel/QualCoder/releases">4.0 beta is in progress</a> and excluded), rather than beta or unreleased features.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Software comparison" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <caption>Scope and installation, reviewed October 25, 2026</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Fleuron</th><th scope="col">Taguette</th><th scope="col">QualCoder</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">License and free use</th><td>MIT; free and open source. Collaboration is paused in 2.8.0, so there are no accounts and no paid tier today. <a href={siteFacts.sources.license}>License</a></td><td>BSD licensed. Local software and the project’s hosted service are free. <a href={`${taguette}install.html`}>Terms and setup</a></td><td>LGPLv3 software. Optional external AI services can have their own terms and costs. <a href={qual}>License and AI setup</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Where it runs</th><td>Native desktop: macOS 14+ and Windows 11 x64 supported. Older systems best effort. <a href="/download/">Support limits</a></td><td>Browser interface, either on a local server for offline use or a shared server. macOS, Windows and Linux. <a href={`${taguette}install.html`}>Installation</a></td><td>Desktop application for Windows, macOS and Linux; installation varies by system. <a href={qual}>Platform instructions</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Material you code</th><td>Text transcripts, including .docx and timestamped text. No direct audio, video or image coding. <a href="/guides/code-interview-transcripts/">Example workflow</a></td><td>Document highlighting and tagging; imports PDF, Word (.docx), text, HTML, ODT, RTF and ebook (EPUB, MOBI) formats. <a href={`${taguette}about.html`}>Supported documents</a></td><td>Text, images, audio and video; media support may need additional software. <a href={qual}>Features and prerequisites</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Getting work out</th><td>HTML/PDF reports and CSV coded passages/matrices. No native NVivo project import. <a href="/guides/code-interview-transcripts/">Sample exports</a></td><td>Exports projects, highlighted extracts, documents and codes. <a href={`${taguette}about.html`}>Export scope</a></td><td>Reporting and project exchange are documented by the project, including a <a href="https://github.com/ccbogel/QualCoder/releases/tag/3.8.2">Taguette project import added in 3.8.2</a>. Cross-tool round trips were not verified here; test your own files before migrating. <a href="https://qualcoder.org/">Manual</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h2>Where each tool keeps your data</h2>
  <p>Fleuron has no collaboration service in 2.8.0: a study is a folder on your computer, and the app makes no sync connection. When collaboration returns, it will share codebook text, labels and coding metadata while excluding transcript text and memo fields, and teammates will still need their own local transcripts. The field-level table is on <a href="/privacy/">the privacy page</a>.</p>
  <p>Taguette supports teamwork through a shared server, either its hosted instance or one you operate. Local single-user installation is also available. Choose the location with your document-handling requirements in mind. <a href={`${taguette}install.html`}>Taguette’s installation guide</a>.</p>
  <p>QualCoder’s documentation includes optional AI integrations. Review the chosen provider and configuration before using those with sensitive material. For teamwork, QualCoder’s documentation describes workflows built around passing project files between coders and merging the results, rather than a live shared workspace. <a href="https://qualcoder.org/doc/en/2.4.-Working-in-a-Team/">QualCoder’s team documentation</a>.</p>
  <h2>“Free” does not remove installation and support work</h2>
  <p>{siteFacts.pricing} Fleuron’s current installers have publisher-verification limitations, and ordinary support runs through public GitHub issues. <a href="/install/">Read the expected warnings and stop conditions</a>.</p>
  <p>Check the other tools’ current installation instructions too: <a href={`${taguette}install.html`}>Taguette’s installation guide</a> and <a href={qual}>QualCoder’s documentation</a>. A local browser interface can still require a running local service; desktop media work can require extra dependencies. A free trial or a license paid for by your institution is different from freely licensed software. This page does not compare commercial prices.</p>
  <h2>Local project files are common, not a Fleuron specialty</h2>
  <p>Keeping research data on your own machine matters to many researchers, and it is well supported across the field. NVivo projects are saved as .nvp files on your computer or a network drive, as its <a href="https://help-nv.qsrinternational.com/20/win/Content/projects-teamwork/create-a-new-project.htm">project creation guide</a> explains, and MAXQDA <a href="https://help.maxqda.com/en/support/solutions/articles/80001146630-where-should-i-save-the-project-file-">recommends local project storage</a> over cloud-synchronized folders. If data locality is a requirement for your project, check each tool’s current guidance directly.</p>
  <p>If you already have a project, list the features and formats it depends on. Exported text and CSV are not a promise that code hierarchies, links, annotations and media will transfer without loss. Keep an untouched original and trial a small migration before committing.</p>
  <h2>Which should you try?</h2>
  <p><strong>Choose Fleuron when</strong> you want a local, single-researcher transcript-coding workspace with evolving codes and memos, and its current platform and signing limits fit your situation. Start with the <a href="/guides/code-interview-transcripts/">synthetic tutorial</a>.</p>
  <p><strong>Choose another tool when</strong> your workflow needs different material types, a browser-based shared workspace, Linux packaging or institutional support arrangements. <a href={taguette}>Taguette</a> is worth trying for document tagging; <a href="https://qualcoder.org/">QualCoder</a> is worth trying for direct media coding. These are workflow judgments, not a ranking or a claim of feature parity.</p>
</GuideLayout>
